1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,287
The Pacific Ocean, the second of

2
00:00:02,307 --> 00:00:05,650
September, 1944.

3
00:00:05,670 --> 00:00:07,410
A few miles off a rocky Japanese island

4
00:00:07,430 --> 00:00:09,514
called Chichi Jima, a twenty-year-old

5
00:00:09,534 --> 00:00:11,840
American pilot sits alone in a small

6
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:12,504
rubber raft.

7
00:00:12,524 --> 00:00:14,829
Minutes earlier he jumped from a burning

8
00:00:14,849 --> 00:00:17,819
plane and struck its tail on the way out.

9
00:00:17,839 --> 00:00:20,144
He paddles as hard as he can,

10
00:00:20,164 --> 00:00:22,801
away from the island, whose guns have just

11
00:00:22,821 --> 00:00:24,150
brought his plane down.

12
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:27,582
Above him, American fighter planes circle

13
00:00:27,602 --> 00:00:29,784
low, to keep Japanese boats away.

14
00:00:29,804 --> 00:00:31,618
Then, a little before noon,

15
00:00:31,638 --> 00:00:34,554
something rises out of the sea a few

16
00:00:34,574 --> 00:00:35,655
hundred yards away.

17
00:00:35,675 --> 00:00:38,224
First a periscope, then a black hull

18
00:00:38,244 --> 00:00:38,958
streaming water.

19
00:00:38,978 --> 00:00:40,793
It is an American submarine,

20
00:00:40,813 --> 00:00:42,628
the U S S Finback.

21
00:00:42,648 --> 00:00:44,829
Sailors run out on the deck,

22
00:00:44,849 --> 00:00:47,031
and up on the conning tower,

23
00:00:47,051 --> 00:00:49,620
someone is holding a small movie camera.

24
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:52,867
The submarine's captain recorded the

25
00:00:52,887 --> 00:00:54,853
rescue in his patrol report.

26
00:00:54,873 --> 00:00:57,635
The page survives in the National Archives.

27
00:00:57,655 --> 00:01:00,813
It gives the time, four minutes to noon,

28
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:03,197
the pilot's name, Lieutenant junior grade

29
00:01:03,217 --> 00:01:03,992
George H.

30
00:01:04,012 --> 00:01:04,389
W.

31
00:01:04,409 --> 00:01:06,376
Bush, and one more line.

32
00:01:06,396 --> 00:01:09,952
Bush told them that he failed to see his

33
00:01:09,972 --> 00:01:12,336
crew's parachutes and believed they had

34
00:01:12,356 --> 00:01:15,180
jumped when plane was still over Chichi

35
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,440
Jima, or they had gone down with plane.

36
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:22,589
Two young men had been in that plane with him.

37
00:01:22,609 --> 00:01:24,132
They never came home.

38
00:01:24,152 --> 00:01:26,447
And on the island behind him,

39
00:01:26,467 --> 00:01:28,762
the Japanese garrison was already holding

40
00:01:28,782 --> 00:01:29,920
American airmen prisoner.

41
00:01:29,940 --> 00:01:32,621
What happened to those men stayed hidden

42
00:01:32,641 --> 00:01:34,570
from their families for decades.

43
00:01:35,975 --> 00:01:38,259
This is the documented story of what

44
00:01:38,279 --> 00:01:40,563
George Herbert Walker Bush really did in

45
00:01:40,583 --> 00:01:43,197
the Second World War, told from his Navy

46
00:01:43,217 --> 00:01:43,526
file:

47
00:01:43,546 --> 00:01:45,172
the squadron's secret action report,

48
00:01:45,192 --> 00:01:46,488
the submarine's patrol report,

49
00:01:46,508 --> 00:01:49,122
the pilot's own logbook in his own hand,

50
00:01:49,142 --> 00:01:52,084
and the record of a war crimes trial held

51
00:01:52,104 --> 00:01:54,388
by the United States Navy on Guam.

52
00:01:54,408 --> 00:01:57,022
Where the papers agree, you will see it.

53
00:01:57,042 --> 00:01:59,675
Where they disagree, you will see that too.

54
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:03,217
You will often read three things about

55
00:02:03,237 --> 00:02:03,870
that morning:

56
00:02:03,890 --> 00:02:05,830
that his plane was on fire,

57
00:02:05,850 --> 00:02:08,770
that he sat in the raft for four hours,

58
00:02:08,790 --> 00:02:11,383
and that his crew bailed out with him.

59
00:02:11,403 --> 00:02:13,343
The file backs one of those.

60
00:02:13,363 --> 00:02:14,650
It quietly corrects another.

61
00:02:14,670 --> 00:02:17,590
And on the third, the file argues with itself.

62
00:02:17,610 --> 00:02:20,550
By the end, you will see which is which.

63
00:02:22,275 --> 00:02:24,975
A man who later became President has a

64
00:02:24,995 --> 00:02:25,995
long paper trail.

65
00:02:26,015 --> 00:02:28,375
But the papers that matter here were

66
00:02:28,395 --> 00:02:31,942
written in 1944, by men who had no idea

67
00:02:31,962 --> 00:02:33,399
who he would become.

68
00:02:33,419 --> 00:02:36,315
To them he was one pilot among thousands,

69
00:02:36,335 --> 00:02:38,137
file number one seven three,

70
00:02:38,157 --> 00:02:39,250
four six four.

71
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:42,429
The first is Form A C A one,

72
00:02:42,449 --> 00:02:44,303
the Aircraft Action Report of Torpedo

73
00:02:44,323 --> 00:02:46,178
Squadron Fifty-One for the second of

74
00:02:46,198 --> 00:02:49,047
September, 1944, stamped secret,

75
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:51,677
five sheets, typed on the carrier a few

76
00:02:51,697 --> 00:02:52,991
days after the mission.

77
00:02:53,011 --> 00:02:55,949
The second is the war patrol report of the

78
00:02:55,969 --> 00:02:57,921
submarine Finback, signed by her captain.

79
00:02:57,941 --> 00:03:00,879
The third is a small brown book with a

80
00:03:00,899 --> 00:03:02,851
cloth spine, the Aviator's Flight Log

81
00:03:02,871 --> 00:03:05,152
Book, where Bush wrote every flight he

82
00:03:05,172 --> 00:03:08,130
made from his first lesson to his last landing.

83
00:03:09,425 --> 00:03:12,244
The fourth file has nothing to do with

84
00:03:12,264 --> 00:03:12,953
Bush himself.

85
00:03:12,973 --> 00:03:15,437
It is eight hundred and twenty-one pages

86
00:03:15,457 --> 00:03:18,276
from the Navy's war crimes trials on Guam,

87
00:03:18,296 --> 00:03:21,095
a case called United States against Yoshio

88
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:22,454
Tachibana and others.

89
00:03:22,474 --> 00:03:24,126
Bush never appears in it.

90
00:03:24,146 --> 00:03:26,802
But it describes the island he was bombing

91
00:03:26,822 --> 00:03:29,144
that morning, and what happened there to

92
00:03:29,164 --> 00:03:31,505
the men who were not picked up.

93
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:35,092
The story starts on the seventh of

94
00:03:35,112 --> 00:03:37,960
December, 1941.

95
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:40,269
Bush was seventeen, a schoolboy at

96
00:03:40,289 --> 00:03:41,809
Phillips Academy in Andover,

97
00:03:41,829 --> 00:03:42,194
Massachusetts.

98
00:03:42,214 --> 00:03:44,118
His family lived in Greenwich,

99
00:03:44,138 --> 00:03:44,503
Connecticut.

100
00:03:44,523 --> 00:03:47,967
He heard the news on a Sunday walk across

101
00:03:47,987 --> 00:03:48,737
the campus.

102
00:03:48,757 --> 00:03:50,662
Fifty years later, as President,

103
00:03:50,682 --> 00:03:54,126
he stood at Pearl Harbor and told a crowd

104
00:03:54,146 --> 00:03:56,840
of veterans what he decided that day.

105
00:03:58,075 --> 00:04:00,087
Like all American kids back then,

106
00:04:00,107 --> 00:04:02,119
I was swept up in it.

107
00:04:02,139 --> 00:04:05,167
I decided that very day to go into the

108
00:04:05,187 --> 00:04:07,199
Navy, to become a Navy pilot.

109
00:04:07,219 --> 00:04:09,231
And so on my eighteenth birthday,

110
00:04:09,251 --> 00:04:13,164
June 12, 1942, I was sworn into the Navy

111
00:04:13,184 --> 00:04:14,375
as a seaman second class.

112
00:04:16,475 --> 00:04:18,855
Seaman second class is the lowest rank in

113
00:04:18,875 --> 00:04:19,455
the Navy.

114
00:04:19,475 --> 00:04:21,555
He had signed up for flight training,

115
00:04:21,575 --> 00:04:24,255
and within a year the Navy would make him

116
00:04:24,275 --> 00:04:25,775
an officer and a pilot.

117
00:04:27,525 --> 00:04:30,180
The Navy turned schoolboys into pilots in

118
00:04:30,200 --> 00:04:31,327
about a year.

119
00:04:31,347 --> 00:04:34,002
Bush started at the preflight school at

120
00:04:34,022 --> 00:04:37,059
the University of North Carolina in Chapel Hill.

121
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:39,735
Then came primary training in a biplane,

122
00:04:39,755 --> 00:04:40,499
the Stearman.

123
00:04:40,519 --> 00:04:43,556
His logbook shows his first solo flight on

124
00:04:43,576 --> 00:04:45,085
the fifteenth of December,

125
00:04:45,105 --> 00:04:47,075
1942.

126
00:04:47,775 --> 00:04:50,437
From there he went to Naval Air Station

127
00:04:50,457 --> 00:04:51,779
Corpus Christi in Texas.

128
00:04:51,799 --> 00:04:55,115
On the ninth of June, 1943,

129
00:04:55,135 --> 00:04:56,995
three days before his nineteenth birthday,

130
00:04:57,015 --> 00:04:59,502
he was commissioned an ensign in the Naval

131
00:04:59,522 --> 00:05:01,382
Reserve and pinned on his wings.

132
00:05:01,402 --> 00:05:04,535
He was one of the youngest pilots in the Navy.

133
00:05:05,875 --> 00:05:09,663
He was sent to fly the Grumman T B F

134
00:05:09,683 --> 00:05:11,568
Avenger, the Navy's torpedo bomber.

135
00:05:11,588 --> 00:05:13,852
He learned it at Fort Lauderdale,

136
00:05:13,872 --> 00:05:14,233
Florida.

137
00:05:14,253 --> 00:05:17,575
In August 1943 he made his first carrier

138
00:05:17,595 --> 00:05:19,955
landings, on the U S S Sable,

139
00:05:19,975 --> 00:05:20,975
a training carrier.

140
00:05:20,995 --> 00:05:23,355
A stamp in his logbook records it:

141
00:05:23,375 --> 00:05:25,395
qualified this date in carrier landings

142
00:05:25,415 --> 00:05:28,115
aboard the U S S Sable, the twenty-fourth

143
00:05:28,135 --> 00:05:30,750
of August, 1943.

144
00:05:31,450 --> 00:05:34,424
That September he was assigned to a

145
00:05:34,444 --> 00:05:36,135
brand-new squadron, Torpedo Squadron

146
00:05:36,155 --> 00:05:39,556
Fifty-One, known in Navy shorthand as V T

147
00:05:39,576 --> 00:05:39,984
fifty-one.

148
00:05:40,004 --> 00:05:42,570
Ensign Bush became its photographic officer.

149
00:05:44,275 --> 00:05:46,795
The squadron's ship was the U S S San

150
00:05:46,815 --> 00:05:49,135
Jacinto, commissioned at Philadelphia on

151
00:05:49,155 --> 00:05:50,315
the fifteenth of November,

152
00:05:50,335 --> 00:05:52,635
1943.

153
00:05:52,655 --> 00:05:54,657
She was a light carrier of the

154
00:05:54,677 --> 00:05:56,391
Independence class, built on a cruiser

155
00:05:56,411 --> 00:05:56,679
hull:

156
00:05:56,699 --> 00:05:58,702
fast, narrow, with a short flight deck

157
00:05:58,722 --> 00:06:00,455
that made every landing a test.

158
00:06:01,775 --> 00:06:03,620
The Avenger carried three men.

159
00:06:03,640 --> 00:06:05,858
The pilot sat up front, alone.

160
00:06:05,878 --> 00:06:09,215
Behind him, in a glass ball turret with a

161
00:06:09,235 --> 00:06:10,334
fifty-caliber machine gun,

162
00:06:10,354 --> 00:06:11,453
sat the gunner.

163
00:06:11,473 --> 00:06:14,064
Down in the belly, below and behind,

164
00:06:14,084 --> 00:06:17,421
sat the radioman, who also fired a gun out

165
00:06:17,441 --> 00:06:18,540
of the tail.

166
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:21,524
The pilot could not see either of them.

167
00:06:21,544 --> 00:06:24,155
He talked to them on the intercom.

168
00:06:25,450 --> 00:06:28,625
Bush's regular crew were two enlisted men.

169
00:06:28,645 --> 00:06:31,510
His radioman was John Delaney,

170
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:33,265
aviation radioman second class,

171
00:06:33,285 --> 00:06:36,336
from Rhode Island, whom everyone called Del.

172
00:06:36,356 --> 00:06:39,870
His turret gunner was Leo Nadeau.

173
00:06:39,890 --> 00:06:42,010
The three of them flew together for months.

174
00:06:43,300 --> 00:06:47,010
In December 1943, before he left for the

175
00:06:47,030 --> 00:06:49,554
Pacific, Bush became engaged to a girl he

176
00:06:49,574 --> 00:06:52,098
had met at a Christmas dance two years

177
00:06:52,118 --> 00:06:53,052
earlier, Barbara Pierce.

178
00:06:53,072 --> 00:06:54,980
He named his planes after her.

179
00:06:56,575 --> 00:06:59,101
San Jacinto reached the war in the spring

180
00:06:59,121 --> 00:07:02,946
of 1944, as part of the fast carrier task force.

181
00:07:02,966 --> 00:07:05,370
Bush's logbook marks his first combat on

182
00:07:05,390 --> 00:07:07,815
the twenty-third of May, 1944,

183
00:07:07,835 --> 00:07:09,225
in two words:

184
00:07:09,245 --> 00:07:10,655
strike, Wake Island.

185
00:07:11,975 --> 00:07:14,152
The same operation took his roommate.

186
00:07:14,172 --> 00:07:16,349
Forty-seven years later, at the memorial

187
00:07:16,369 --> 00:07:19,279
in Honolulu where the names of the missing

188
00:07:19,299 --> 00:07:22,595
are carved in stone, Bush told the story himself.

189
00:07:23,950 --> 00:07:25,633
My roommate aboard the carrier San

190
00:07:25,653 --> 00:07:28,472
Jacinto, C V L thirty, was a guy named Jim

191
00:07:28,492 --> 00:07:31,026
Wykes, and as we were about to go into

192
00:07:31,046 --> 00:07:32,445
combat for the first time,

193
00:07:32,465 --> 00:07:33,865
a strike over Wake Island,

194
00:07:33,885 --> 00:07:36,703
Jim Wykes and his crew were sent out on a

195
00:07:36,723 --> 00:07:38,710
search mission from which they never returned.

196
00:07:40,650 --> 00:07:43,300
In June the carriers moved on the

197
00:07:43,320 --> 00:07:43,681
Marianas:

198
00:07:43,701 --> 00:07:44,826
Saipan, Tinian, Guam.

199
00:07:44,846 --> 00:07:47,877
On the fifteenth of June, Bush wrote in

200
00:07:47,897 --> 00:07:50,166
his logbook, in a different ink,

201
00:07:50,186 --> 00:07:51,330
Marines invaded Saipan.

202
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:55,373
Four days later came the largest carrier

203
00:07:55,393 --> 00:07:58,164
battle of the war, the Battle of the

204
00:07:58,184 --> 00:07:58,862
Philippine Sea.

205
00:07:58,882 --> 00:08:00,956
American fighter pilots called it the

206
00:08:00,976 --> 00:08:02,352
Great Marianas Turkey Shoot.

207
00:08:02,372 --> 00:08:06,210
For Bush it was the day he lost his first plane.

208
00:08:07,525 --> 00:08:09,395
His logbook entry for the nineteenth of

209
00:08:09,415 --> 00:08:12,620
June, 1944, is written in red ink.

210
00:08:12,640 --> 00:08:15,385
Avenger number two five one two three,

211
00:08:15,405 --> 00:08:19,945
one and a half hours, crew Delaney and Nadeau.

212
00:08:19,965 --> 00:08:22,745
Emergency crash landing in sea near Guam.

213
00:08:22,765 --> 00:08:25,545
Self and two crewmen picked up by

214
00:08:25,565 --> 00:08:26,365
destroyer Bronson.

215
00:08:27,825 --> 00:08:30,562
He set the crippled plane down on the

216
00:08:30,582 --> 00:08:32,975
water, and all three men got out.

217
00:08:32,995 --> 00:08:35,387
The destroyer U S S Clarence K.

218
00:08:35,407 --> 00:08:36,766
Bronson picked them up.

219
00:08:36,786 --> 00:08:39,868
For the next four days the logbook has the

220
00:08:39,888 --> 00:08:41,936
same two words on each line:

221
00:08:41,956 --> 00:08:42,756
sack time.

222
00:08:43,950 --> 00:08:46,188
Then it was back to work:

223
00:08:46,208 --> 00:08:49,574
strikes on Guam, on Rota, on the Palau islands.

224
00:08:49,594 --> 00:08:51,455
On the twenty-fifth of July,

225
00:08:51,475 --> 00:08:54,089
Bush and another pilot were credited with

226
00:08:54,109 --> 00:08:57,099
sinking a small Japanese cargo ship near Palau.

227
00:08:57,119 --> 00:09:00,486
On the first of August he was promoted to

228
00:09:00,506 --> 00:09:01,615
lieutenant junior grade.

229
00:09:01,635 --> 00:09:03,872
He had flown dozens of missions.

230
00:09:03,892 --> 00:09:06,150
He had already been lucky once.

231
00:09:07,825 --> 00:09:10,657
At the end of August the carriers turned

232
00:09:10,677 --> 00:09:13,153
north, toward a group of small volcanic

233
00:09:13,173 --> 00:09:15,649
islands six hundred miles south of Tokyo:

234
00:09:15,669 --> 00:09:16,719
the Bonin Islands.

235
00:09:16,739 --> 00:09:20,205
The Japanese called them Ogasawara.

236
00:09:20,225 --> 00:09:22,225
The largest was Chichi Jima,

237
00:09:22,245 --> 00:09:24,265
and it mattered for one reason.

238
00:09:24,285 --> 00:09:26,645
It had radio stations, and the Japanese

239
00:09:26,665 --> 00:09:29,365
used it as a link for communications and

240
00:09:29,385 --> 00:09:32,325
supplies to their garrison on Iwo Jima,

241
00:09:32,345 --> 00:09:34,305
less than two hundred miles further south.

242
00:09:35,625 --> 00:09:37,823
The island was a fortress.

243
00:09:37,843 --> 00:09:41,245
An Army brigade under Major General Yoshio

244
00:09:41,265 --> 00:09:43,965
Tachibana dug guns into the hills around

245
00:09:43,985 --> 00:09:46,525
the harbor of Futami.

246
00:09:46,545 --> 00:09:48,783
The Navy had a base there under Rear

247
00:09:48,803 --> 00:09:51,205
Admiral Kunizo Mori.

248
00:09:51,225 --> 00:09:53,258
The prosecutor's opening statement at the

249
00:09:53,278 --> 00:09:55,996
later trial on Guam records that the first

250
00:09:56,016 --> 00:09:58,555
American air raids over Chichi Jima came

251
00:09:58,575 --> 00:10:01,225
in June of 1944.

252
00:10:01,925 --> 00:10:04,518
The same record shows that by the time

253
00:10:04,538 --> 00:10:06,152
Bush flew over the island,

254
00:10:06,172 --> 00:10:08,438
the garrison had already taken American airmen.

255
00:10:08,458 --> 00:10:10,725
On or about the fourth of July,

256
00:10:10,745 --> 00:10:14,805
1944, an aviator was captured on Ani Jima,

257
00:10:14,825 --> 00:10:18,125
a small island next to Chichi Jima.

258
00:10:18,145 --> 00:10:19,885
He was Lloyd Richard Woellhof,

259
00:10:19,905 --> 00:10:21,307
aviation radioman second class,

260
00:10:21,327 --> 00:10:24,152
from Clay Center, Kansas, the gunner of a

261
00:10:24,172 --> 00:10:26,305
dive bomber from the carrier Yorktown.

262
00:10:27,575 --> 00:10:30,238
The record says he was kept in a garage

263
00:10:30,258 --> 00:10:31,430
for about a month.

264
00:10:31,450 --> 00:10:33,815
Then, on or about the seventh of August,

265
00:10:33,835 --> 00:10:37,505
1944, on orders of General Tachibana,

266
00:10:37,525 --> 00:10:39,497
Woellhof and another American,

267
00:10:39,517 --> 00:10:41,822
whose name we do not know,

268
00:10:41,842 --> 00:10:44,534
were killed with bayonets by Japanese soldiers.

269
00:10:44,554 --> 00:10:47,633
Nobody in Bush's squadron knew any of this.

270
00:10:47,653 --> 00:10:50,939
To the pilots, Chichi Jima was a target on

271
00:10:50,959 --> 00:10:52,495
a chart, with very heavy flak.

272
00:10:54,125 --> 00:10:56,090
The Navy did know one thing:

273
00:10:56,110 --> 00:10:58,075
pilots shot down near Japanese islands

274
00:10:58,095 --> 00:11:01,052
could be saved, if someone was waiting for them.

275
00:11:01,072 --> 00:11:02,706
So when the carriers struck,

276
00:11:02,726 --> 00:11:05,353
submarines lay off the islands on what was

277
00:11:05,373 --> 00:11:06,365
called lifeguard duty.

278
00:11:07,650 --> 00:11:08,950
On the sixteenth of August,

279
00:11:08,970 --> 00:11:13,146
1944, the submarine Finback left Majuro in

280
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:15,638
the Marshall Islands on her tenth war

281
00:11:15,658 --> 00:11:18,842
patrol, with orders to stand by off the Bonins.

282
00:11:18,862 --> 00:11:20,978
She had a new captain, Lieutenant

283
00:11:20,998 --> 00:11:22,046
Commander Robert R.

284
00:11:22,066 --> 00:11:24,538
Williams Junior, who had taken command two

285
00:11:24,558 --> 00:11:25,358
weeks earlier.

286
00:11:26,500 --> 00:11:28,016
On the first of September,

287
00:11:28,036 --> 00:11:29,860
the first day of the strikes,

288
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:31,704
Finback picked up her first airmen:

289
00:11:31,724 --> 00:11:33,855
Ensign Thomas Keene and two enlisted men

290
00:11:33,875 --> 00:11:35,084
from the carrier Franklin,

291
00:11:35,104 --> 00:11:38,520
found in a raft near Chichi Jima.

292
00:11:38,540 --> 00:11:40,747
The same day, Bush flew his first mission

293
00:11:40,767 --> 00:11:42,620
against the island, with Delaney and

294
00:11:42,640 --> 00:11:44,627
Nadeau, and came back safely.

295
00:11:44,647 --> 00:11:45,847
His logbook says:

296
00:11:45,867 --> 00:11:49,120
strike on Bonin Islands, two point seven hours.

297
00:11:50,850 --> 00:11:53,740
On the morning of the second of September,

298
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:55,923
Torpedo Squadron Fifty-One drew a second

299
00:11:55,943 --> 00:11:58,470
strike on the island, called Strike Baker.

300
00:11:58,490 --> 00:12:00,653
The target was a radio station:

301
00:12:00,673 --> 00:12:03,563
four buildings and a tower on the hills

302
00:12:03,583 --> 00:12:04,655
above the harbor.

303
00:12:04,675 --> 00:12:06,838
Four Avengers would make the attack,

304
00:12:06,858 --> 00:12:08,657
each with four five-hundred-pound bombs,

305
00:12:08,677 --> 00:12:10,840
escorted by twelve Hellcat fighters and

306
00:12:10,860 --> 00:12:13,022
eight Helldiver dive bombers from the

307
00:12:13,042 --> 00:12:13,842
carrier Enterprise.

308
00:12:15,125 --> 00:12:17,605
That morning Bush's crew changed.

309
00:12:17,625 --> 00:12:21,236
Leo Nadeau, his gunner, stayed on the ship.

310
00:12:21,256 --> 00:12:24,554
In his place in the turret was an officer,

311
00:12:24,574 --> 00:12:26,396
Lieutenant junior grade William Gardner

312
00:12:26,416 --> 00:12:28,239
White, the squadron's ordnance officer,

313
00:12:28,259 --> 00:12:28,976
called Ted.

314
00:12:28,996 --> 00:12:31,556
He was twenty-six, a Yale man from

315
00:12:31,576 --> 00:12:34,505
Minnesota, and he had asked to come along

316
00:12:34,525 --> 00:12:36,716
to see the island for himself.

317
00:12:36,736 --> 00:12:40,425
Del Delaney took his usual seat as radioman.

318
00:12:41,125 --> 00:12:42,675
According to the action report,

319
00:12:42,695 --> 00:12:45,816
the four planes left San Jacinto at a

320
00:12:45,836 --> 00:12:47,363
quarter past seven.

321
00:12:47,383 --> 00:12:50,972
In the lead was the squadron's commanding

322
00:12:50,992 --> 00:12:53,034
officer, Lieutenant Commander D.

323
00:12:53,054 --> 00:12:53,549
J.

324
00:12:53,569 --> 00:12:54,065
Melvin.

325
00:12:54,085 --> 00:12:56,643
Bush was third in line.

326
00:12:56,663 --> 00:12:58,725
The weather was good.

327
00:13:00,425 --> 00:13:02,822
At about twenty-five minutes past eight by

328
00:13:02,842 --> 00:13:04,893
the squadron's clock, the four Avengers

329
00:13:04,913 --> 00:13:07,309
reached the island at eight thousand feet

330
00:13:07,329 --> 00:13:09,381
and pushed over into their dive,

331
00:13:09,401 --> 00:13:10,762
from south to north.

332
00:13:10,782 --> 00:13:12,488
The report describes the anti-aircraft

333
00:13:12,508 --> 00:13:14,904
fire as very intense, heavy and medium,

334
00:13:14,924 --> 00:13:16,305
and of accurate variety.

335
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:20,600
Melvin and the second pilot put all eight

336
00:13:20,620 --> 00:13:22,715
of their bombs on the station.

337
00:13:22,735 --> 00:13:24,830
Then the report turns to Bush,

338
00:13:24,850 --> 00:13:28,002
and this is the line his whole Navy legend

339
00:13:28,022 --> 00:13:28,708
rests on.

340
00:13:28,728 --> 00:13:29,765
The report says:

341
00:13:29,785 --> 00:13:31,880
Lieutenant junior grade Bush was piloting

342
00:13:31,900 --> 00:13:33,995
the third plane over the target.

343
00:13:34,015 --> 00:13:36,815
Bush's plane was hit in the engine shortly

344
00:13:36,835 --> 00:13:39,635
after final push over at eight thousand feet.

345
00:13:39,655 --> 00:13:42,455
In spite of this hit which caused his

346
00:13:42,475 --> 00:13:44,922
engine to smoke and catch on fire,

347
00:13:44,942 --> 00:13:47,038
Lieutenant junior grade Bush continued in

348
00:13:47,058 --> 00:13:49,858
his dive, releasing his bombs on the radio

349
00:13:49,877 --> 00:13:51,640
station to score damaging hits.

350
00:13:52,975 --> 00:13:55,125
Forty-eight years later, in a White House

351
00:13:55,145 --> 00:13:57,605
interview filmed for a history of the war,

352
00:13:57,625 --> 00:13:59,175
Bush described that dive himself.

353
00:14:00,525 --> 00:14:02,245
About halfway down the run,

354
00:14:02,265 --> 00:14:05,726
I was hit, and you could feel the plane go

355
00:14:05,746 --> 00:14:08,163
push forward like that, up and forward,

356
00:14:08,183 --> 00:14:10,947
and then suddenly it was all engulfed in

357
00:14:10,967 --> 00:14:13,384
flames, and I finished my bombing run,

358
00:14:13,404 --> 00:14:16,517
but by the time I pulled out over the

359
00:14:16,537 --> 00:14:18,625
ocean, the plane was really burning.

360
00:14:19,475 --> 00:14:21,726
In other words, with his engine burning,

361
00:14:21,746 --> 00:14:23,348
he did not pull out.

362
00:14:23,368 --> 00:14:25,294
He held a diving plane steady,

363
00:14:25,314 --> 00:14:28,214
through the flak, all the way down to the

364
00:14:28,234 --> 00:14:30,485
release point, and dropped his four bombs

365
00:14:30,505 --> 00:14:33,405
on the target before he tried to save himself.

366
00:14:33,425 --> 00:14:35,351
That is what his Distinguished Flying

367
00:14:35,371 --> 00:14:36,325
Cross was for.

368
00:14:36,345 --> 00:14:37,947
Its citation, signed that December,

369
00:14:37,967 --> 00:14:38,920
says the same:

370
00:14:38,940 --> 00:14:41,840
in spite of smoke and flames from the fire

371
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:44,455
in his plane, he continued in his dive.

372
00:14:46,175 --> 00:14:48,450
After the bombs fell, the report says,

373
00:14:48,470 --> 00:14:51,072
Bush turned sharply to the east to clear

374
00:14:51,092 --> 00:14:53,039
the island, smoke and flames enveloping

375
00:14:53,059 --> 00:14:54,678
his engine and spreading aft,

376
00:14:54,698 --> 00:14:55,989
his plane losing altitude.

377
00:14:56,009 --> 00:14:58,940
He told his skipper on the radio that he

378
00:14:58,960 --> 00:15:00,251
had to bail out.

379
00:15:00,271 --> 00:15:02,873
He also ordered his crew to bail out.

380
00:15:02,893 --> 00:15:04,840
In the language of the day,

381
00:15:04,860 --> 00:15:07,155
he told them to hit the silk.

382
00:15:08,425 --> 00:15:10,480
Then the report says something that would

383
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:13,148
be argued over for the rest of his life.

384
00:15:13,168 --> 00:15:15,519
At a point about nine miles north-east of

385
00:15:15,539 --> 00:15:18,165
the small island of Minami Jima,

386
00:15:18,185 --> 00:15:21,360
Bush and one other person were seen to

387
00:15:21,380 --> 00:15:24,156
bail out, from about three thousand feet.

388
00:15:24,176 --> 00:15:25,354
Bush's chute opened.

389
00:15:25,374 --> 00:15:27,750
The chute of the other person,

390
00:15:27,770 --> 00:15:30,546
either Lieutenant junior grade White or J.

391
00:15:30,566 --> 00:15:30,945
L.

392
00:15:30,965 --> 00:15:34,185
Delaney, who bailed out, did not open.

393
00:15:35,450 --> 00:15:38,403
The report adds a careful note in brackets.

394
00:15:38,423 --> 00:15:41,375
Bush had not yet been returned to the

395
00:15:41,395 --> 00:15:43,233
squadron by the rescue submarine,

396
00:15:43,253 --> 00:15:45,091
so this information is incomplete.

397
00:15:45,111 --> 00:15:48,064
And it closes with the words every family

398
00:15:48,084 --> 00:15:48,435
dreaded:

399
00:15:48,455 --> 00:15:50,623
while White and Delaney are reported

400
00:15:50,643 --> 00:15:52,840
missing in action, it is believed that

401
00:15:52,860 --> 00:15:53,810
both were killed.

402
00:15:55,150 --> 00:15:56,961
In the same 1992 interview,

403
00:15:56,981 --> 00:15:59,210
Bush told the bailout this way.

404
00:16:00,425 --> 00:16:02,092
Told them to bail out.

405
00:16:02,112 --> 00:16:05,129
And from there on, a little unclear as to

406
00:16:05,149 --> 00:16:07,829
what happened, but it turns out that one

407
00:16:07,849 --> 00:16:10,528
of them got out, and his chute streamed,

408
00:16:10,548 --> 00:16:13,585
his chute were up, and the other did not.

409
00:16:14,375 --> 00:16:17,235
Bush himself had hit the tail of his plane

410
00:16:17,255 --> 00:16:18,195
as he jumped.

411
00:16:18,215 --> 00:16:21,075
He later said he pulled the ripcord too early.

412
00:16:21,095 --> 00:16:23,955
He came down in the sea with a cut

413
00:16:23,975 --> 00:16:25,235
forehead, swallowed salt water,

414
00:16:25,255 --> 00:16:27,175
found the raft, and climbed in.

415
00:16:28,825 --> 00:16:31,082
From the air, the fighters of Fighting

416
00:16:31,102 --> 00:16:32,709
Squadron Twenty watched him paddle,

417
00:16:32,729 --> 00:16:35,311
and stayed over his raft to protect him,

418
00:16:35,331 --> 00:16:36,937
as the action report says.

419
00:16:36,957 --> 00:16:39,214
Somewhere off the island, the Finback got

420
00:16:39,234 --> 00:16:40,034
the call.

421
00:16:41,175 --> 00:16:44,133
Her patrol report gives the times in its

422
00:16:44,153 --> 00:16:45,250
own time zone.

423
00:16:45,270 --> 00:16:47,112
At nine thirty-three, the submarine

424
00:16:47,132 --> 00:16:50,090
received word of a plane down nine miles

425
00:16:50,110 --> 00:16:52,675
north-east of Minami Jima.

426
00:16:52,695 --> 00:16:54,355
She started around the southern end of

427
00:16:54,375 --> 00:16:57,015
Chichi Jima, keeping at least seven and a

428
00:16:57,035 --> 00:16:58,955
half miles off the coast and its guns.

429
00:17:00,350 --> 00:17:03,342
At four minutes to noon, the boat came up

430
00:17:03,362 --> 00:17:04,346
beside the raft.

431
00:17:04,366 --> 00:17:06,780
A sailor named Donnet Kohler helped pull

432
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,068
the pilot aboard, and someone on the

433
00:17:09,088 --> 00:17:10,375
conning tower filmed it.

434
00:17:10,395 --> 00:17:11,682
This is that film.

435
00:17:11,702 --> 00:17:13,642
It runs less than a minute,

436
00:17:13,662 --> 00:17:16,930
and it is the only moving picture of the moment.

437
00:17:18,225 --> 00:17:20,788
How long was Bush in the water?

438
00:17:20,808 --> 00:17:23,003
Popular accounts say about four hours.

439
00:17:23,023 --> 00:17:24,848
The two reports, read together,

440
00:17:24,868 --> 00:17:28,170
suggest it was closer to two and a half.

441
00:17:28,190 --> 00:17:31,491
Either way, he was alone, and he was close

442
00:17:31,511 --> 00:17:34,075
enough to the island to see it.

443
00:17:34,095 --> 00:17:37,027
That is the second claim from the beginning.

444
00:17:37,047 --> 00:17:39,242
The four hours is the story.

445
00:17:39,262 --> 00:17:41,845
The papers point to a shorter wait.

446
00:17:43,125 --> 00:17:44,799
I remember being sick.

447
00:17:44,819 --> 00:17:47,764
I remember being scared, and I remember

448
00:17:47,784 --> 00:17:50,306
wondering what happened to the crewmen

449
00:17:50,326 --> 00:17:52,000
that were with me.

450
00:17:52,020 --> 00:17:54,985
That preoccupied a lot of my thinking.

451
00:17:55,855 --> 00:17:57,635
The submarine did not leave.

452
00:17:57,655 --> 00:18:00,515
Her report says that once Bush was aboard,

453
00:18:00,535 --> 00:18:03,035
she commenced a search of the area,

454
00:18:03,055 --> 00:18:06,275
on the chance his crew had jumped over water.

455
00:18:06,295 --> 00:18:07,375
She found nothing.

456
00:18:09,080 --> 00:18:11,128
So, what does the file really say happened

457
00:18:11,148 --> 00:18:14,020
to Ted White and Del Delaney?

458
00:18:14,040 --> 00:18:16,200
The honest answer is that the papers of

459
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,960
1944 do not agree with each other.

460
00:18:20,205 --> 00:18:22,438
The squadron report, written from what the

461
00:18:22,458 --> 00:18:25,012
other pilots saw, says Bush and one other

462
00:18:25,032 --> 00:18:27,264
person jumped, and the second chute failed.

463
00:18:27,284 --> 00:18:29,195
The submarine's report, written the same

464
00:18:29,215 --> 00:18:30,804
day from Bush's own words,

465
00:18:30,824 --> 00:18:33,378
says he never saw any parachutes at all,

466
00:18:33,398 --> 00:18:35,952
and believed his crew had jumped over the

467
00:18:35,972 --> 00:18:38,225
island, or gone down with the plane.

468
00:18:39,530 --> 00:18:41,670
And there is a third witness.

469
00:18:41,690 --> 00:18:43,830
That same afternoon, Finback rescued a

470
00:18:43,850 --> 00:18:46,350
fighter pilot under fire off the next

471
00:18:46,370 --> 00:18:48,150
island, Haha Jima:

472
00:18:48,170 --> 00:18:50,843
Ensign James Beckman, who held on to the

473
00:18:50,863 --> 00:18:52,862
periscope while the submarine towed him

474
00:18:52,882 --> 00:18:53,872
out to sea.

475
00:18:53,892 --> 00:18:56,901
Once aboard, Beckman added a line to the record.

476
00:18:56,921 --> 00:18:59,930
The report says it was known that only one

477
00:18:59,950 --> 00:19:01,970
man had parachuted from Bush's plane.

478
00:19:03,305 --> 00:19:05,885
Bush's own logbook says the least of all.

479
00:19:05,905 --> 00:19:08,810
On the line for the second of September he

480
00:19:08,830 --> 00:19:11,410
wrote the plane's number, four six two one

481
00:19:11,430 --> 00:19:13,035
four, one point seven hours,

482
00:19:13,055 --> 00:19:15,279
the names Delaney and Lieutenant junior

483
00:19:15,299 --> 00:19:16,871
grade White, and then:

484
00:19:16,891 --> 00:19:19,657
crash landing in sea near Bonin Island,

485
00:19:19,677 --> 00:19:20,453
enemy action.

486
00:19:20,473 --> 00:19:24,035
It was the standard phrase for a lost plane.

487
00:19:24,055 --> 00:19:26,045
Neither body was ever found.

488
00:19:27,730 --> 00:19:29,860
Finback could not take Bush home.

489
00:19:29,880 --> 00:19:32,726
She was on patrol, and she stayed on

490
00:19:32,746 --> 00:19:35,234
patrol, with five rescued airmen aboard as

491
00:19:35,254 --> 00:19:35,592
passengers.

492
00:19:35,612 --> 00:19:38,459
So for about a month, the torpedo bomber

493
00:19:38,479 --> 00:19:40,270
pilot lived on a submarine.

494
00:19:41,730 --> 00:19:42,731
He stood watches.

495
00:19:42,751 --> 00:19:45,114
He was there when the boat was

496
00:19:45,134 --> 00:19:47,496
depth-charged, and when she sank two small

497
00:19:47,516 --> 00:19:48,177
Japanese freighters.

498
00:19:48,197 --> 00:19:49,879
Forty-eight years later, accepting his

499
00:19:49,899 --> 00:19:51,240
party's nomination for President,

500
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:53,963
he described standing watch at four in the

501
00:19:53,983 --> 00:19:56,345
morning on the bridge of the Finback,

502
00:19:56,365 --> 00:19:59,068
looking out at the dark sea and thinking

503
00:19:59,088 --> 00:20:00,790
about friends he had lost.

504
00:20:02,105 --> 00:20:04,684
From the submarine, he wrote to his parents.

505
00:20:04,704 --> 00:20:07,283
The letter is quoted in his own collection

506
00:20:07,303 --> 00:20:07,933
of papers.

507
00:20:07,953 --> 00:20:11,182
He wrote that there was no sign of Del or

508
00:20:11,202 --> 00:20:12,157
Ted anywhere around.

509
00:20:12,177 --> 00:20:15,406
He said that he sat in his raft and sobbed

510
00:20:15,426 --> 00:20:18,005
for a while, and that he felt terribly

511
00:20:18,025 --> 00:20:19,325
responsible for their fate.

512
00:20:20,605 --> 00:20:22,005
On the thirtieth of September,

513
00:20:22,025 --> 00:20:25,247
1944, Finback tied up at Midway.

514
00:20:25,267 --> 00:20:28,013
A Navy photographer lined up the five

515
00:20:28,033 --> 00:20:29,988
rescued airmen with the submarine's

516
00:20:30,008 --> 00:20:31,173
officers on deck.

517
00:20:31,193 --> 00:20:33,939
Bush is kneeling, second from the left.

518
00:20:33,959 --> 00:20:36,704
In the back row stands Ensign Bill

519
00:20:36,724 --> 00:20:39,470
Edwards, the officer usually named as the

520
00:20:39,490 --> 00:20:41,465
man who filmed the rescue.

521
00:20:43,155 --> 00:20:46,101
From Midway, Bush was flown to Pearl Harbor.

522
00:20:46,121 --> 00:20:47,954
Then, instead of going home,

523
00:20:47,974 --> 00:20:50,179
he went back to his squadron.

524
00:20:50,199 --> 00:20:52,403
He hitched rides across the Pacific,

525
00:20:52,423 --> 00:20:54,915
by Johnston Island, Kwajalein,

526
00:20:54,935 --> 00:20:57,355
Guam and Ulithi, and rejoined Torpedo

527
00:20:57,375 --> 00:21:00,605
Squadron Fifty-One in November 1944.

528
00:21:01,305 --> 00:21:03,288
He flew eight more combat missions,

529
00:21:03,308 --> 00:21:05,290
including strikes on Japanese shipping in

530
00:21:05,310 --> 00:21:06,959
Manila Bay in the Philippines.

531
00:21:06,979 --> 00:21:09,962
By the end of its tour, the squadron had

532
00:21:09,982 --> 00:21:11,985
lost about half of its pilots.

533
00:21:13,355 --> 00:21:15,965
In all, his file credits him with

534
00:21:15,985 --> 00:21:17,092
fifty-eight combat missions.

535
00:21:17,112 --> 00:21:19,345
He was awarded the Distinguished Flying

536
00:21:19,365 --> 00:21:21,224
Cross and three Air Medals,

537
00:21:21,244 --> 00:21:23,863
and San Jacinto received the Presidential

538
00:21:23,883 --> 00:21:24,775
Unit Citation.

539
00:21:24,795 --> 00:21:28,735
On the sixth of January, 1945,

540
00:21:28,755 --> 00:21:30,475
he married Barbara Pierce in Rye,

541
00:21:30,495 --> 00:21:32,235
New York, in his Navy uniform.

542
00:21:33,555 --> 00:21:36,471
He spent the rest of the war training a

543
00:21:36,491 --> 00:21:39,081
new torpedo squadron for the invasion of Japan.

544
00:21:39,101 --> 00:21:41,691
The last page of totals in his logbook

545
00:21:41,711 --> 00:21:42,017
reads:

546
00:21:42,037 --> 00:21:43,974
one thousand two hundred and twenty-eight

547
00:21:43,994 --> 00:21:45,931
point three hours in the air,

548
00:21:45,951 --> 00:21:48,235
and one hundred and twenty-six carrier landings.

549
00:21:49,430 --> 00:21:51,693
If you want more stories like this,

550
00:21:51,713 --> 00:21:54,410
told from the documents, subscribe to WWII

551
00:21:54,430 --> 00:21:54,818
Frontlines.

552
00:21:54,838 --> 00:21:58,510
And now, back to the island Bush left behind.

553
00:22:00,230 --> 00:22:03,525
In February 1945, the American fleet came

554
00:22:03,545 --> 00:22:06,349
back to the Bonins, this time to cover the

555
00:22:06,369 --> 00:22:08,570
invasion of Iwo Jima.

556
00:22:08,590 --> 00:22:10,120
On the eighteenth of February,

557
00:22:10,140 --> 00:22:12,282
carrier planes struck Chichi Jima again,

558
00:22:12,302 --> 00:22:13,550
and several were lost.

559
00:22:14,855 --> 00:22:17,622
An Avenger from the carrier Randolph went

560
00:22:17,642 --> 00:22:19,214
down near the island.

561
00:22:19,234 --> 00:22:21,603
Its pilot, Ensign Floyd Ewing Hall,

562
00:22:21,623 --> 00:22:23,915
and his radioman, Marve Mershon,

563
00:22:23,935 --> 00:22:25,308
aviation radioman third class,

564
00:22:25,328 --> 00:22:28,093
were pulled from the water and taken prisoner.

565
00:22:28,113 --> 00:22:29,485
Their gunner, Glenn Frazier,

566
00:22:29,505 --> 00:22:32,271
hid on the island for several days before

567
00:22:32,291 --> 00:22:33,335
he was caught.

568
00:22:34,580 --> 00:22:36,403
Another Avenger, from the carrier

569
00:22:36,423 --> 00:22:38,984
Bennington, was hit by a second American

570
00:22:39,004 --> 00:22:40,459
plane in the air.

571
00:22:40,479 --> 00:22:42,671
Its pilot ditched and was rescued.

572
00:22:42,691 --> 00:22:45,620
His two crewmen came down by parachute on

573
00:22:45,640 --> 00:22:47,520
Ani Jima and were captured:

574
00:22:47,540 --> 00:22:49,620
Grady York, aviation ordnanceman third

575
00:22:49,640 --> 00:22:51,300
class, from Jacksonville, Florida,

576
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:53,820
and James Wesley Dye, radioman third

577
00:22:53,840 --> 00:22:55,500
class, from Mount Ephraim,

578
00:22:55,520 --> 00:22:56,360
New Jersey.

579
00:22:57,655 --> 00:22:59,760
Then, on the twenty-third of February,

580
00:22:59,780 --> 00:23:01,884
a Marine fighter pilot from the

581
00:23:01,904 --> 00:23:03,655
Bennington, Second Lieutenant Warren Earl

582
00:23:03,675 --> 00:23:05,071
Vaughn of Childress, Texas,

583
00:23:05,091 --> 00:23:06,841
was shot down and captured.

584
00:23:06,861 --> 00:23:10,028
On the island he was guarded by a young

585
00:23:10,048 --> 00:23:12,507
Japanese radio operator who had been born

586
00:23:12,527 --> 00:23:15,075
in Hawaii, Nobuaki Iwatake.

587
00:23:15,095 --> 00:23:17,475
The two became friends.

588
00:23:19,255 --> 00:23:21,180
The trial record describes what followed

589
00:23:21,200 --> 00:23:23,449
in the plain language of a prosecutor,

590
00:23:23,469 --> 00:23:25,394
and it is hard to read.

591
00:23:25,414 --> 00:23:27,987
These are the facts, without the worst of

592
00:23:28,007 --> 00:23:28,807
the detail.

593
00:23:29,930 --> 00:23:32,190
Hall and Mershon were taken to General

594
00:23:32,210 --> 00:23:35,074
Tachibana's headquarters and tied to trees.

595
00:23:35,094 --> 00:23:37,336
During a bombing raid, two Japanese

596
00:23:37,356 --> 00:23:40,351
officers began taking them to an air-raid shelter.

597
00:23:40,371 --> 00:23:42,989
The record says that on the way,

598
00:23:43,009 --> 00:23:45,250
they were stopped by Major General

599
00:23:45,270 --> 00:23:47,981
Tachibana and ordered not to give the

600
00:23:48,001 --> 00:23:49,610
prisoners shelter, food nor water.

601
00:23:50,880 --> 00:23:52,780
On or about the twenty-third of February,

602
00:23:52,800 --> 00:23:55,634
1945, Mershon was beheaded.

603
00:23:55,654 --> 00:23:58,888
York was put to death with bamboo spears

604
00:23:58,908 --> 00:23:59,702
and bayonets.

605
00:23:59,722 --> 00:24:01,736
Frazier was beaten to death.

606
00:24:01,756 --> 00:24:05,398
Dye was kept alive for a while to help

607
00:24:05,418 --> 00:24:07,432
decode messages, and then killed.

608
00:24:07,452 --> 00:24:09,466
Hall was executed in March,

609
00:24:09,486 --> 00:24:12,980
on the orders of Major Sueo Matoba,

610
00:24:13,000 --> 00:24:14,515
a battalion commander.

611
00:24:14,535 --> 00:24:17,605
So, in March, was Warren Vaughn.

612
00:24:18,305 --> 00:24:20,758
The trial record also states that after

613
00:24:20,778 --> 00:24:23,232
some of the executions, the bodies were

614
00:24:23,252 --> 00:24:26,058
cut open, and that flesh was taken from

615
00:24:26,078 --> 00:24:28,532
them and eaten by Japanese officers at

616
00:24:28,552 --> 00:24:30,652
their headquarters, with sake to drink.

617
00:24:30,672 --> 00:24:32,290
Major Matoba ordered it.

618
00:24:32,310 --> 00:24:34,464
The prosecution said the general himself

619
00:24:34,484 --> 00:24:37,745
had asked for meat at one of those parties.

620
00:24:39,130 --> 00:24:40,970
In all, eight American airmen were

621
00:24:40,990 --> 00:24:43,562
captured on and around Chichi Jima and

622
00:24:43,582 --> 00:24:44,347
killed there.

623
00:24:44,367 --> 00:24:46,700
Not one of them came home.

624
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:49,446
Their families were told only that they

625
00:24:49,466 --> 00:24:50,266
were missing.

626
00:24:52,005 --> 00:24:54,681
When the war ended, the island's officers

627
00:24:54,701 --> 00:24:56,222
had a story ready.

628
00:24:56,242 --> 00:24:58,533
American investigators were told that the

629
00:24:58,553 --> 00:25:01,229
captured airmen had been killed in air

630
00:25:01,249 --> 00:25:03,540
raids, or sent away to Japan.

631
00:25:03,560 --> 00:25:06,700
Major Yoshitaka Horie, the general's chief

632
00:25:06,720 --> 00:25:09,125
of staff, later testified that Tachibana

633
00:25:09,145 --> 00:25:11,536
had ordered the records on the American

634
00:25:11,556 --> 00:25:12,356
flyers destroyed.

635
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:15,482
The story did not hold.

636
00:25:15,502 --> 00:25:18,942
Among the people of the islands were a few

637
00:25:18,962 --> 00:25:21,249
families descended from early Western settlers.

638
00:25:21,269 --> 00:25:23,940
One of them, Fred Savory, told the

639
00:25:23,960 --> 00:25:25,478
Americans what he knew.

640
00:25:25,498 --> 00:25:27,400
On the twenty-first of December,

641
00:25:27,420 --> 00:25:31,240
1945, a Marine colonel, Presley Rixey,

642
00:25:31,260 --> 00:25:35,080
opened a board of investigation on Chichi Jima.

643
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:37,083
Over the next months he questioned more

644
00:25:37,103 --> 00:25:38,820
than a hundred and twenty witnesses.

645
00:25:40,280 --> 00:25:41,600
On the seventh of January,

646
00:25:41,620 --> 00:25:45,736
1946, Major Horie admitted that his first

647
00:25:45,756 --> 00:25:47,392
statement had been false.

648
00:25:47,412 --> 00:25:49,509
Then Major Matoba testified.

649
00:25:49,529 --> 00:25:51,146
He admitted ordering executions,

650
00:25:51,166 --> 00:25:54,419
and he admitted taking part in the cannibalism.

651
00:25:54,439 --> 00:25:58,102
He said he had been a madman because of

652
00:25:58,122 --> 00:25:58,940
the war.

653
00:26:00,605 --> 00:26:03,388
The cases were tried by a United States

654
00:26:03,408 --> 00:26:05,139
Navy military commission on Guam,

655
00:26:05,159 --> 00:26:07,942
where the Navy held its war crimes trials

656
00:26:07,962 --> 00:26:09,343
for the Pacific islands.

657
00:26:09,363 --> 00:26:12,365
The main case opened in August 1946,

658
00:26:12,385 --> 00:26:14,832
with fourteen defendants, among them

659
00:26:14,852 --> 00:26:19,155
General Tachibana, Admiral Mori and Major Matoba.

660
00:26:19,855 --> 00:26:22,924
The charges were murder, violation of the

661
00:26:22,944 --> 00:26:26,895
laws and customs of war, and neglect of duty.

662
00:26:26,915 --> 00:26:28,661
There were thirty-eight specifications.

663
00:26:28,681 --> 00:26:30,867
One problem had no precedent.

664
00:26:30,887 --> 00:26:33,515
There was no law, anywhere in

665
00:26:33,535 --> 00:26:35,280
international law, against cannibalism.

666
00:26:35,300 --> 00:26:38,810
So the Navy lawyers charged it as the

667
00:26:38,830 --> 00:26:40,595
prevention of honorable burial.

668
00:26:41,955 --> 00:26:44,228
The prosecutor, Lieutenant Daniel Flynn of

669
00:26:44,248 --> 00:26:46,903
the Naval Reserve, explained it in his

670
00:26:46,923 --> 00:26:47,667
opening argument.

671
00:26:47,687 --> 00:26:49,195
Several of the specifications,

672
00:26:49,215 --> 00:26:51,870
he said, allege the commission of acts

673
00:26:51,890 --> 00:26:54,927
which are so revolting to the human mind

674
00:26:54,947 --> 00:26:57,602
that man long ago decided it unnecessary

675
00:26:57,622 --> 00:26:59,915
to legislate directly against their commission.

676
00:27:01,330 --> 00:27:02,210
He went on:

677
00:27:02,230 --> 00:27:04,910
there is no doubt in our minds that the

678
00:27:04,930 --> 00:27:06,710
series of offenses these defendants have

679
00:27:06,730 --> 00:27:08,510
committed rank with the most startling

680
00:27:08,530 --> 00:27:10,910
ever to be heard in a criminal court.

681
00:27:10,930 --> 00:27:13,610
It is our hope that never again will the

682
00:27:13,630 --> 00:27:16,030
like of these cases have to be presented.

683
00:27:17,405 --> 00:27:19,800
It was not a secret trial.

684
00:27:19,820 --> 00:27:23,725
In September 1946, Time magazine reported

685
00:27:23,745 --> 00:27:26,187
Matoba's confession to the American public.

686
00:27:26,207 --> 00:27:28,846
But the Navy did not share the details

687
00:27:28,866 --> 00:27:31,173
with the families of the dead men,

688
00:27:31,193 --> 00:27:33,500
and the story faded from the newspapers

689
00:27:33,520 --> 00:27:34,320
within weeks.

690
00:27:35,930 --> 00:27:38,968
Of the fourteen defendants in the first

691
00:27:38,988 --> 00:27:41,153
case, thirteen were found guilty.

692
00:27:41,173 --> 00:27:44,606
General Tachibana was sentenced to death.

693
00:27:44,626 --> 00:27:47,067
Admiral Mori received a life sentence.

694
00:27:47,087 --> 00:27:50,211
He was later tried by a Dutch court for

695
00:27:50,231 --> 00:27:51,031
other crimes.

696
00:27:52,330 --> 00:27:53,790
On the twenty-fourth of September,

697
00:27:53,810 --> 00:27:58,102
1947, Yoshio Tachibana was hanged on Guam

698
00:27:58,122 --> 00:28:00,070
and buried in an unmarked grave.

699
00:28:00,090 --> 00:28:02,792
Matoba was hanged too.

700
00:28:02,812 --> 00:28:05,275
The Navy's own newspaper reported that

701
00:28:05,295 --> 00:28:07,344
five Japanese had been executed.

702
00:28:07,364 --> 00:28:10,241
The sources differ on the other names,

703
00:28:10,261 --> 00:28:12,330
so we leave them out.

704
00:28:13,530 --> 00:28:15,680
Two years before, on the third of

705
00:28:15,700 --> 00:28:18,925
September, 1945, Tachibana had climbed

706
00:28:18,945 --> 00:28:21,010
aboard the American destroyer Dunlap off

707
00:28:21,030 --> 00:28:23,816
Chichi Jima and surrendered the Bonin Islands.

708
00:28:23,836 --> 00:28:26,247
A Navy cameraman filmed the Japanese

709
00:28:26,267 --> 00:28:28,273
officers climbing aboard and signing.

710
00:28:28,293 --> 00:28:31,514
It was one day after the first anniversary

711
00:28:31,534 --> 00:28:34,370
of the morning Bush was shot down.

712
00:28:36,005 --> 00:28:39,338
In 1988, when Bush was running for

713
00:28:39,358 --> 00:28:41,876
President, the story of the second of

714
00:28:41,896 --> 00:28:42,964
September came back.

715
00:28:42,984 --> 00:28:45,140
A former Torpedo Squadron Fifty-One gunner

716
00:28:45,160 --> 00:28:47,913
named Chester Mierzejewski told a New York

717
00:28:47,933 --> 00:28:50,168
newspaper that he had been in the turret

718
00:28:50,188 --> 00:28:52,424
of a plane flying about a hundred feet

719
00:28:52,444 --> 00:28:53,270
ahead of Bush's.

720
00:28:53,290 --> 00:28:55,807
He said the plane had not been on fire,

721
00:28:55,827 --> 00:28:58,062
that he saw only a puff of smoke,

722
00:28:58,082 --> 00:29:00,036
and that only one man got out.

723
00:29:00,056 --> 00:29:02,291
He believed Bush could have tried to land

724
00:29:02,311 --> 00:29:04,285
on the water and save his crew.

725
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:07,563
Bush's campaign called the claim absurd.

726
00:29:07,583 --> 00:29:09,899
Bush himself always said the plane was

727
00:29:09,919 --> 00:29:12,903
burning and that he had done what he had

728
00:29:12,923 --> 00:29:13,571
to do.

729
00:29:13,591 --> 00:29:16,241
In 2003 he told an interviewer that the

730
00:29:16,261 --> 00:29:19,245
plane was burning, and that he knew he had

731
00:29:19,265 --> 00:29:20,600
done the right thing.

732
00:29:21,855 --> 00:29:23,930
So what does the file show?

733
00:29:23,950 --> 00:29:27,155
The two Navy documents of 1944,

734
00:29:27,175 --> 00:29:29,904
the action report and the medal citation,

735
00:29:29,924 --> 00:29:33,046
both say the engine was hit and burning.

736
00:29:33,066 --> 00:29:35,795
The action report, written from the other

737
00:29:35,815 --> 00:29:38,151
pilots' reports, says smoke and flames.

738
00:29:38,171 --> 00:29:40,115
The medal citation says fire.

739
00:29:40,135 --> 00:29:44,021
Mierzejewski's account comes from one man,

740
00:29:44,041 --> 00:29:45,164
forty-four years later.

741
00:29:45,184 --> 00:29:48,615
On the fire, the documents are on Bush's side.

742
00:29:49,905 --> 00:29:52,492
On the parachutes, the file cannot settle

743
00:29:52,512 --> 00:29:54,726
it, because its own pages disagree:

744
00:29:54,746 --> 00:29:57,333
one other man jumped, no parachutes seen,

745
00:29:57,353 --> 00:29:58,823
only one man jumped.

746
00:29:58,843 --> 00:30:01,902
Whether Ted White or Del Delaney ever left

747
00:30:01,922 --> 00:30:03,995
the plane, no record can say.

748
00:30:04,015 --> 00:30:06,805
That is the honest edge of the evidence.

749
00:30:08,155 --> 00:30:10,935
So here are the three claims from the

750
00:30:10,955 --> 00:30:13,035
beginning, as the file reads them.

751
00:30:13,055 --> 00:30:13,735
The fire:

752
00:30:13,755 --> 00:30:16,185
confirmed, by the action report and the

753
00:30:16,205 --> 00:30:19,215
medal citation of 1944.

754
00:30:19,235 --> 00:30:21,396
The four hours in the raft:

755
00:30:21,416 --> 00:30:23,577
shortened, by the submarine's own clock.

756
00:30:23,597 --> 00:30:25,031
The crew bailing out:

757
00:30:25,051 --> 00:30:27,595
the papers disagree, and they always will.

758
00:30:29,305 --> 00:30:31,547
Ted White and John Delaney were officially

759
00:30:31,567 --> 00:30:33,865
declared dead on the third of September,

760
00:30:33,885 --> 00:30:36,385
1945.

761
00:30:36,405 --> 00:30:39,067
Their names are carved on the Courts of

762
00:30:39,087 --> 00:30:41,078
the Missing at the National Memorial

763
00:30:41,098 --> 00:30:43,090
Cemetery of the Pacific in Honolulu.

764
00:30:43,110 --> 00:30:46,005
Leo Nadeau, the gunner who stayed on the

765
00:30:46,025 --> 00:30:47,505
ship that morning, survived the war.

766
00:30:48,780 --> 00:30:51,416
Finback was decommissioned in 1950 and

767
00:30:51,436 --> 00:30:55,120
sold for scrap in 1959.

768
00:30:55,140 --> 00:30:58,240
San Jacinto was scrapped in 1971.

769
00:30:58,260 --> 00:31:02,360
Nobuaki Iwatake, Warren Vaughn's guard and

770
00:31:02,380 --> 00:31:04,880
friend, took the first name Warren after

771
00:31:04,900 --> 00:31:05,600
the war.

772
00:31:05,620 --> 00:31:07,060
He died in 2012.

773
00:31:08,455 --> 00:31:09,715
On the second of September,

774
00:31:09,735 --> 00:31:12,387
1984, forty years to the day,

775
00:31:12,407 --> 00:31:15,158
Bush took out his old logbook and had a

776
00:31:15,178 --> 00:31:17,621
page signed by the men who were there:

777
00:31:17,641 --> 00:31:19,468
Finback officers, the airmen she rescued,

778
00:31:19,488 --> 00:31:20,700
and his squadron mates.

779
00:31:20,720 --> 00:31:22,875
The signatures are still in the book.

780
00:31:24,130 --> 00:31:26,154
In June 2002, at seventy-eight,

781
00:31:26,174 --> 00:31:29,530
Bush went back to Chichi Jima.

782
00:31:29,550 --> 00:31:31,955
He saw the ruins of the radio station.

783
00:31:31,975 --> 00:31:34,381
He dropped wreaths on the sea for Ted

784
00:31:34,401 --> 00:31:39,030
White and Del Delaney, and he met Warren Iwatake.

785
00:31:39,050 --> 00:31:41,353
A year later the author James Bradley

786
00:31:41,373 --> 00:31:43,676
published the story of the eight captured

787
00:31:43,696 --> 00:31:45,336
airmen, and their surviving relatives

788
00:31:45,356 --> 00:31:46,995
learned, for the first time,

789
00:31:47,015 --> 00:31:48,655
what had happened to them.

790
00:31:48,675 --> 00:31:51,330
Most of their parents had died without knowing.

791
00:31:52,605 --> 00:31:55,587
George Herbert Walker Bush died on the

792
00:31:55,607 --> 00:31:57,302
thirtieth of November, 2018,

793
00:31:57,322 --> 00:31:58,180
at ninety-four.

794
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:00,676
Torpedo Squadron Fifty-One,

795
00:32:00,696 --> 00:32:03,300
the San Jacinto, the Finback,

796
00:32:03,320 --> 00:32:05,398
the carriers that struck the Bonins:

797
00:32:05,418 --> 00:32:07,846
thousands of American families have a man

798
00:32:07,866 --> 00:32:08,896
in this story.

799
00:32:08,916 --> 00:32:11,344
If your father, grandfather or uncle flew

800
00:32:11,364 --> 00:32:13,792
from those carriers, served on a lifeguard

801
00:32:13,812 --> 00:32:16,590
submarine, or was one of the airmen lost

802
00:32:16,610 --> 00:32:20,204
over Chichi Jima, tell us his name in the

803
00:32:20,224 --> 00:32:20,528
comments.

804
00:32:20,548 --> 00:32:22,472
And if you read these documents

805
00:32:22,492 --> 00:32:25,064
differently than we do, tell us that too.

806
00:32:25,084 --> 00:32:27,332
They are public records, and anyone can

807
00:32:27,352 --> 00:32:28,152
check them.

808
00:32:29,630 --> 00:32:33,350
Chichi Jima went back to Japan in 1968,

809
00:32:33,370 --> 00:32:35,585
after twenty-three years under the United

810
00:32:35,605 --> 00:32:36,330
States Navy.

811
00:32:36,350 --> 00:32:39,310
Today it belongs to the city of Tokyo.

812
00:32:39,330 --> 00:32:43,270
Boats come and go in the harbor of Futami,

813
00:32:43,290 --> 00:32:45,667
and in a bay on the island's south side,

814
00:32:45,687 --> 00:32:47,798
the wreck of a Japanese wartime cargo ship

815
00:32:47,818 --> 00:32:49,150
still lies in the shallows.

816
00:32:50,430 --> 00:32:52,243
Somewhere off the north-east coast,

817
00:32:52,263 --> 00:32:54,870
about nine miles from Minami Jima,

818
00:32:54,890 --> 00:32:57,555
is the place where an Avenger with three

819
00:32:57,575 --> 00:32:59,569
men aboard went into the sea.

820
00:32:59,589 --> 00:33:01,247
There is no marker there.

821
00:33:01,267 --> 00:33:03,931
There is only the line in the logbook,

822
00:33:03,951 --> 00:33:05,610
in the pilot's own hand:

823
00:33:05,630 --> 00:33:07,959
crash landing in sea near Bonin Island,

824
00:33:07,979 --> 00:33:08,650
enemy action.

